                      (   unit_tests.asm):00333         ; System/Console I/O test harness (glossary section 3.1). Half
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00334         ; of this section's 12 words are deliberately NOT tested here,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00335         ; for reasons confirmed by reading their own implementations
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00336         ; directly, not assumed from the glossary alone:
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00337         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00338         ; KEY genuinely spins forever without real input (both its
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00339         ; interrupt-driven and polled variants branch back to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00340         ; themselves - "BEQ KEY" - until a character arrives); ACCEPT
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00341         ; calls KEY directly in its own main loop, and EXPECT/QUERY both
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00342         ; call ACCEPT - all four inherit the same block. None of these
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00343         ; can complete during automated, headless boot-time testing,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00344         ; where no real input will ever arrive.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00345         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00346         ; ABORT falls straight through into QUIT, which resets S to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00347         ; RP0 - discarding this entire call chain, including the path
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00348         ; back to TSTRUNNER - and enters the interpreter's own top-level
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00349         ; loop. Calling either directly would hijack the boot sequence
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00350         ; entirely, not just fail one test.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00351         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00352         ; EMIT/TYPE/CR/SPACE/SPACES are safe and already proven so - this
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00353         ; whole test framework has used TYPE and CR (via TSTREPORT)
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00354         ; thousands of times already across every prior test group
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00355         ; without incident. Each test here verifies the actual queuing
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00356         ; mechanism (OUTHEAD/OUTBUF), not just "didn't crash" - reading
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00357         ; the real character(s) back out of the output ring buffer to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00358         ; confirm they were genuinely queued, not just that the call
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00359         ; returned.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00360         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00361         
                      (   unit_tests.asm):00362         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
